WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:01.767
Hi.

2
00:00:01.693 --> 00:00:06.278
I'm Doug Thielen, Chief Information Officer at
Kestrel Falls Community Hospital.

3
00:00:06.278 --> 00:00:08.835
That letter from Plexmoor was
addressed to me,

4
00:00:08.835 --> 00:00:11.078
so let me tell you how it
landed this morning.

5
00:00:11.078 --> 00:00:13.508
We've run Plexmoor since 2012.

6
00:00:13.508 --> 00:00:15.062
That's fifteen years.

7
00:00:15.062 --> 00:00:19.597
About nineteen hundred people use it every day,
in the hospital, our ten clinics,

8
00:00:19.597 --> 00:00:20.887
and the ambulance service.

9
00:00:20.887 --> 00:00:23.943
It holds about four hundred ten
thousand patient records.

10
00:00:23.943 --> 00:00:29.338
And it talks to thirty-eight other systems: the
lab, imaging, pharmacy, billing,

11
00:00:29.338 --> 00:00:32.205
the ambulance, the state
immunization registry,

12
00:00:32.205 --> 00:00:35.082
the regional health information
exchange, and more.

13
00:00:35.082 --> 00:00:36.758
My first reaction?

14
00:00:36.758 --> 00:00:41.123
This is a big IT project, and my team
knows how to run IT projects.

15
00:00:41.123 --> 00:00:41.723
We have nine EHR analysts.

16
00:00:41.123 --> 00:00:45.600
We pick a replacement, we move the data,
we rebuild the interfaces,

17
00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:49.215
we train people, and we switch over.

18
00:00:49.215 --> 00:00:52.392
My honest estimate for a hospital our size is
twelve to twenty-four months from

19
00:00:52.392 --> 00:00:53.830
signing a contract to go-live.

20
00:00:53.830 --> 00:00:56.282
That's an estimate, not a promise.

21
00:00:56.282 --> 00:00:59.581
The date I keep looking at is
December thirty-first, 2028.

22
00:00:59.581 --> 00:01:01.133
That's when standard support ends.

23
00:01:01.133 --> 00:01:06.098
After that, if something breaks, nobody at
Plexmoor picks up the phone.

24
00:01:06.098 --> 00:01:07.581
Money matters too.

25
00:01:07.581 --> 00:01:11.403
Greg and I put the numbers on the
fact sheet for you.

26
00:01:11.403 --> 00:01:15.417
Our whole EHR costs about six point eight
million dollars a year to run,

27
00:01:15.417 --> 00:01:17.589
and two point two million of that is
the Plexmoor support fee.

28
00:01:17.589 --> 00:01:22.357
Out of a three hundred seventy-five
million dollar operating budget,

29
00:01:22.357 --> 00:01:23.912
that's under two percent.

30
00:01:23.912 --> 00:01:27.802
A new system will cost a lot more than
one year of that, up front.

31
00:01:27.802 --> 00:01:30.449
Laura tells me this is bigger than IT.

32
00:01:30.449 --> 00:01:33.666
She says it touches every patient
and every clinician,

33
00:01:33.666 --> 00:01:35.581
and that the board will see it that way.

34
00:01:35.581 --> 00:01:36.738
Maybe she's right.

35
00:01:36.738 --> 00:01:40.296
I'll be honest: I'm stretched thin, my
team is stretched thin,

36
00:01:40.296 --> 00:01:44.197
and I'd rather we spent our time picking a
vendor than writing memos about it.

37
00:01:44.197 --> 00:01:45.719
So here's what would help me.

38
00:01:45.719 --> 00:01:48.293
Read the letter closely, every line.

39
00:01:48.293 --> 00:01:51.735
Tell Laura what it actually says,
not what we wish it said.

40
00:01:51.735 --> 00:01:55.316
If something in it is unclear, say
so instead of guessing.

41
00:01:55.316 --> 00:01:57.274
The board meets February sixteenth,

42
00:01:57.274 --> 00:02:01.612
and I don't want us telling them something
the letter doesn't support.

43
00:02:01.612 --> 00:02:02.212
Thanks.

44
00:02:01.612 --> 00:02:04.520
I'll be in the server room if
anyone needs me.

